window.SKIM_PAPERS["2312.03732"] = {"concepts": [{"id": "lora-adapter-form", "name": "The LoRA adapter and its scaling factor", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "LoRA finetunes a frozen model by adding a trainable low-rank product BA, multiplied by a scaling factor, to selected weight matrices. Conventional implementations set that factor to alpha divided by the rank r.", "explanation": "Take a linear sub-module of a pretrained network with weights W in R^(d2 x d1) and bias b, mapping x_out = W x_in + b. LoRA freezes W and b and augments the sub-module with two trainable matrices, A in R^(r x d1) and B in R^(d2 x r), plus a positive scalar gamma_r, giving x_out = (W + gamma_r B A) x_in + b. The rank r is the shared inner dimension and is normally set far below d1 and d2, so B A is a rank-at-most-r update; A and B are the only parameters optimised during finetuning. Initialisation is asymmetric: B starts at all zeros, so the adapter contributes nothing at step zero, while entries of A are drawn iid with mean 0 and a variance sigma_A that does not depend on r. Adapters are typically placed on all non-LayerNorm linear sub-modules of the transformer (attention projections and feed-forward MLPs), though attention-only placement is also common. After training, W + gamma_r B A is collapsed into a single matrix and stored in place of W, so a finetuned model costs nothing extra at inference no matter what rank was used; rank costs memory and compute only during training. The scaling factor gamma_r exists to compensate for how the magnitude of the product B A grows with r, and in the practiced LoRA method it is set to gamma_r = alpha / r, where alpha is a hyperparameter chosen by the user. This is why LoRA configurations are always reported as a pair, a rank r and an alpha: neither number determines the size of the update on its own.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 2.1 (Low-Rank Adapters), equations (1) and (2), and the paragraph defining gamma_r = alpha/r.", "ownerPaper": "2312.03732", "sources": {"paperId": "2312.03732", "note": "Section 2.1 (Low-Rank Adapters), equations (1) and (2), and the paragraph defining gamma_r = alpha/r.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "rank-stabilized-adapters", "name": "Rank-stabilized adapters", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "An adapter is called rank-stabilized if changing r leaves both the size of what it outputs and the size of the gradients passing back through it unchanged in order of magnitude. The paper proves this holds only when the scaling factor shrinks like 1 over the square root of r.", "explanation": "The paper's Definition 3.1 states two conditions on an adapter gamma_r B A, both in terms of order of magnitude as r grows (written Theta_r(1), meaning bounded above and below by constants independent of r). Forward condition: if the adapter's inputs are iid with m-th moment Theta_r(1) per entry, the m-th moment of its outputs is also Theta_r(1) per entry. Backward condition: if the loss gradients arriving at the adapter's outputs are Theta_r(1) per entry, the gradients it passes to its inputs are also Theta_r(1) per entry. Theorem 3.2 says that, in expectation over the initialisation and assuming gamma_r tends to 0 as r grows, adapters are rank-stabilized if and only if gamma_r is in Theta_r(1/sqrt(r)); otherwise learning is unstable or collapsing for large enough r. The proof (Appendix A) tracks SGD updates from the B = 0 initialisation and finds that after n steps the expected adapter equals roughly -gamma_r^2 r sigma_A eta times a sum of outer products of past gradients and inputs, using E(A_0^T A_0) = r sigma_A I. Both the forward outputs and the backward gradients therefore scale as Theta_r(gamma_r^2 r), and the only way for that to be Theta_r(1) is gamma_r proportional to 1/sqrt(r). Substituting the conventional choice gamma_r = alpha/r gives gamma_r^2 r = alpha^2 / r, which goes to zero: the adapter's effective contribution and its gradients shrink as rank rises. The paper is explicit that the theorem is about stability versus collapse in the limit of r and says nothing about whether features learned at different ranks are better or worse when learning is stable, which is why it treats the practical payoff as something that needs experimental checking.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3, Definition 3.1 and Theorem 3.2 (equations 4 and 5); full proof in Appendix A, equations (6)-(11).", "ownerPaper": "2312.03732", "sources": {"paperId": "2312.03732", "note": "Section 3, Definition 3.1 and Theorem 3.2 (equations 4 and 5); full proof in Appendix A, equations (6)-(11).", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "rslora-scaling-factor", "name": "rsLoRA: the alpha over square-root-r scaling factor", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "rsLoRA is LoRA with one change: the adapter scaling factor becomes alpha divided by the square root of the rank instead of alpha divided by the rank. Nothing else about the adapter, its initialisation, or its merging at inference changes.", "explanation": "The rank-stabilized LoRA (rsLoRA) method sets gamma_r = alpha / sqrt(r), with alpha left as a free hyperparameter exactly as in LoRA. The theory pins down only the dependence on r; the constant alpha is not prescribed by the paper, so the same alpha means a different multiplier under the two conventions. Concretely, with r = 32 and alpha = 64, standard LoRA scales the adapter by 64/32 = 2, while rsLoRA scales it by 64/sqrt(32), roughly 11.3 - about 5.7 times larger, and the ratio widens as r grows (at r = 2048 it is a factor of about 45). This matters when reading a reported LoRA configuration: a rank/alpha pair is only interpretable once you know which scaling convention the trainer used, and libraries commonly expose rsLoRA as a boolean flag alongside the rank and alpha settings. The adapter form is unchanged, so B still starts at zero, the finetuned adapter is still merged into W + gamma_r B A after training, and inference cost is identical for any rank. The paper also shows the correction has to live in the scaling factor rather than the initialisation: scaling A's initialisation by 1/sqrt(r) with no scaling factor made training unstable at rank 2048, and combining that initialisation with LoRA's 1/r factor reproduced LoRA's flat, sub-optimal behaviour. Scaling factors on either side of 1/sqrt(r) fail in the predicted directions - 1/r^(1/4) is too large and destabilises, 1/r^2 collapses learning even harder than 1/r.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 3 (equation 4, the rsLoRA definition); Appendix B.3 (initialisation-only scaling) and B.6 (other scaling factors, equations 12 and 13).", "ownerPaper": "2312.03732", "sources": {"paperId": "2312.03732", "note": "Section 3 (equation 4, the rsLoRA definition); Appendix B.3 (initialisation-only scaling) and B.6 (other scaling factors, equations 12 and 13).", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "rank-compute-performance-tradeoff", "name": "Gradient collapse and the rank/performance trade-off", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "Under LoRA's alpha/r scaling, gradient norms shrink as rank rises and finetuning loss is roughly the same at rank 4 as at rank 2048. Under rsLoRA the gradients stay in the same order of magnitude and higher ranks train to lower loss.", "explanation": "The paper finetunes Llama 2 on 20,000 examples of the OpenOrca instruction-tuning dataset with AdamW at learning rate 5e-5, sweeping ranks r in {4, 8, 32, 128, 512, 2048}, with adapters on all linear attention and MLP sub-modules. With standard LoRA the training perplexity curves for all ranks converge to nearly the same value, some large ranks slightly worse, while the average parameter gradient norm falls by orders of magnitude as rank rises - the collapse the theory predicts. With rsLoRA the gradient norms start at the same value for every rank and stay within about an order of magnitude of each other through training, and perplexity improves monotonically with rank. The author reads this as an explanation for the widespread belief that very low ranks (4, 8, 16) suffice for LoRA: that conclusion was drawn from experiments where rank 64 showed no gain, which the collapsing scaling factor would produce regardless of the true value of extra capacity. A learning-rate sweep for rank-4 LoRA is offered as a control - its best perplexity, 1.863 at learning rate 5e-4, does not reach rsLoRA's 1.836 at rank 2048 with the default learning rate - which the paper takes as evidence that the larger gamma_r is not merely acting as a learning-rate boost. The same pattern is reported with SGD, with GPT-J finetuned on the GSM8k grade-school maths dataset using Adafactor, and with attention-only adapter placement. The practical framing is a compute/performance trade-off: because rank costs training memory and compute but nothing at inference, rsLoRA lets a practitioner spend the largest rank their memory budget allows. The evidence is a small number of runs on training perplexity rather than downstream task benchmarks, so the claim is about finetuning loss, not demonstrated downstream quality.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 4 and Figures 2 and 3; ablations in Appendix B.1 (SGD), B.2 (GPT-J / GSM8k / Adafactor), B.4 (learning-rate sweep), B.5 (attention-only).", "ownerPaper": "2312.03732", "sources": {"paperId": "2312.03732", "note": "Section 4 and Figures 2 and 3; ablations in Appendix B.1 (SGD), B.2 (GPT-J / GSM8k / Adafactor), B.4 (learning-rate sweep), B.5 (attention-only).", "sections": [], "pages": []}}], "items": [], "edges": [], "themes": [], "pages": [], "narrative": null, "insights": null, "summary": null, "reading": [], "refs": {"accessed": []}, "regions": [], "pdfPages": 0, "readerKind": "pdf", "sections": []};
